※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⎕narrow(EXPR, ⌜ENUM⌝)` reads a number as one of an enumeration's values.
※
※ **An enumeration is a narrower type than the one it is held as**, so making one
※ out of a number is a narrowing like any other and fails the same way.  What
※ differs is that its values are not a range: the number has to *be* one of them,
※ and `⎕narrowing.absent` is what says it was not.  That is the same question the
※ other three reasons answer -- does this number fit this type -- asked of a type
※ whose values have gaps between them.
※
※ **There is no other way to do it.**  A program that reads a number off a
※ device, a protocol or the kernel and wants it as a named thing had to write a
※ comparison per value; `std` did, eighteen of them, before this.
※
※ **No branch.**  An enumeration is held as its number, so where the number is
※ one of them the answer is the same bits; what has to be worked out is only
※ whether it is one, which is one comparison per value folded together.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, whose `TryFrom<u8>` a derive writes out and whose error is
※ the number back again; **Zig**, whose `@enumFromInt` checks in a safe build and
※ does not in a fast one, which is the thing this language does not do; **C#**,
※ whose `Enum.IsDefined` is a library call over reflection; **C**, where an
※ enumeration holds any number of its underlying type and the question cannot be
※ asked at all.

enum Code:
    ok = 0 ;
    again = 5 ;
    gone = 32

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ one of them, from a wider type
    let a: Code = ⎕narrow(5i64, ⌜Code⌝) ?? Code.ok
    ※ and from a narrower one
    let b: Code = ⎕narrow(32u8, ⌜Code⌝) ?? Code.ok
    if a ≠ Code.again:
        1u6
    elif b ≠ Code.gone:
        2u6
    else:
        ※ a number the enumeration does not name, which says which way it did
        ※ not fit
        match ⎕narrow(99i64, ⌜Code⌝):
            Code:
                3u6
            ⊥(why):
                if why = ⎕narrowing.absent: 0u6 else: 4u6
